For the given array of integers, the best sorting algorithm is Quick Sort. Quick Sort is an in-place sorting algorithm which has a time complexity of O(nlogn). It has the advantage of being faster than most other comparison based sorting algorithms. It uses an approach called the "divide and conquer" approach, which divides the array into two halves and then recursively sorts each half. In order to sort this array of integers, Quick Sort would look like this: 

def quickSort(arr): 
    if len(arr) <= 1: 
        return arr 
    else: 
        pivot = arr[0] # select pivot 
        left = [x for x in arr[1:] if x < pivot] # elements less than pivot 
        right = [x for x in arr[1:] if x >= pivot] # elements greater than pivot 
        return quickSort(left) + [pivot] + quickSort(right) 

